LEAPMOTOR_USER=your@email.com
LEAPMOTOR_PASS=yourpassword
# The operation PIN for every car without its own (Settings → Vehicle stores a car's own)
LEAPMOTOR_PIN=0000
DB_PATH=leapmotor_mate.db
# Optional: origins allowed to submit cross-origin write requests (e.g. a reverse proxy that
# rewrites Host, so the browser's real origin looks foreign to us). Comma-separated, full
# origin with scheme. Does NOT allow framing — see MATE_FRAME_ANCESTORS for that.
# MATE_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://mate.example.com

# Optional: origins allowed to embed Mate in a frame, e.g. a Home Assistant dashboard you
# control. Comma-separated; each entry must be exactly scheme://host[:port] — no path, no
# wildcards, ASCII only. Separate from MATE_TRUSTED_ORIGINS: an origin that may embed Mate is
# not automatically one that may also submit write requests, and vice versa.
#
# Spell out the port if it isn't the scheme's default (80 for http, 443 for https) — a CSP
# host-source with no port matches ONLY that default port, so a typical
# http://homeassistant.local:8123 needs the :8123, or the embed is refused with no obvious
# reason why. And note the browser enforces mixed content before any of this: an https://
# dashboard can't frame a plain http:// Mate at all, whatever you put here.
# Through hass_ingress (its default `ingress` mode) Mate is served from Home Assistant's own
# address, so that is the origin to name — and every other one you open Home Assistant with
# (a Nabu Casa URL, say), comma-separated.
# MATE_FRAME_ANCESTORS=http://homeassistant.local:8123

# Optional: names Mate answers on WITHOUT a password, besides the ones a home network uses (an IP
# address, localhost, a name without dots, .local, .lan, .home, .home.arpa, .internal). Comma-
# separated host names, port optional, e.g. the public name of a reverse proxy, a DuckDNS name or
# a Tailscale *.ts.net name. Without a password Mate refuses every other name: a page on someone
# else's site can make its own name point at your Mate (DNS rebinding) and drive it. With a
# password set this is not needed. The hosts in MATE_TRUSTED_ORIGINS are already included.
# MATE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=mate.example.com
